  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "[P1] Parse `cached.value` instead of the `{ value, stale }` wrapper",
      "body": "Requires any cache hit. `Cache.get` now returns `{ value: string; stale: boolean } | null` (`src/cache.ts:5-6`), so on a hit `cached` is a truthy object and `src/profile.ts:19` executes `JSON.parse(cached)` with a non-string. This is a TypeScript type error, and in emitted JavaScript `JSON.parse` coerces the object to `\"[object Object]\"` and throws `SyntaxError`, so `loadProfile` rejects instead of returning the cached profile. Fix: `if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached.value);`.",
      "severity": "P1",
      "location": {
        "path": "src/profile.ts",
        "side": "RIGHT",
        "start": 19,
        "end": 19
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "[P3] Confirm intended: new `stale` flag has no consumer",
      "body": "The new `stale` metadata returned by `readWithMetadata` (`src/cache.ts:5-6`) is read by nothing — `loadProfile` is the only caller of `cache.get` and ignores it, so stale entries are served exactly like fresh ones. If refresh-on-stale handling is planned for a follow-up, fine; otherwise the flag is dead surface on this contract.",
      "severity": "P3",
      "location": {
        "path": "src/cache.ts",
        "side": "RIGHT",
        "start": 5,
        "end": 6
      }
    }
  ],
